[in] if non-zero, then indicates the maximum time (in nanoseconds) to yield before returning ZE_RESULT_SUCCESS or ZE_RESULT_NOT_READY;
if zero, then immediately returns the status of the command queue;
if `UINT64_MAX`, then function will not return until complete or device is lost.
Due to external dependencies, timeout may be rounded to the closest value allowed by the accuracy of those dependencies.

details:
This is a special-case system level barrier that can be used to ensure global observability of writes; 
typically needed after a producer (e.g., NIC) performs direct writes to the device's memory (e.g., Direct RDMA writes).
This is typically required when the memory corresponding to the writes is subsequently accessed from a remote device.

